many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy  and practice i've created this series to  provide short answers to the most common  recurring or confusing questions about  stoicism  one common question that arises when it  comes to stoicism  is what are the first steps to becoming  stoic and so there's a lot to say in  response to this  excellent question i think it's  excellent in that it gives us a lot of  opportunities to explore some things  that need to  to be examined and it also  represents a sort of diligence  and wish to improve oneself using  something that perhaps one doesn't know  that much about  so the answers that i'm going to provide  here  some of them are positive you definitely  want to do these sorts of things  some of them are negative meaning that  i'm saying ah you probably want to avoid  this because  it's actually going to be a distraction  or it's going to be too superficial  or it's going to take you off the path  and set you behind  in some respects and i will say right  off the bat that  what i'm saying here these are my views  about it and there  there are some other experts on stoicism  who probably  would disagree with at least some of  this and that's that's perhaps a good  starting point there is no one single  stoic path and there is no one single  thing that you  absolutely have to begin with whether  it's a concept or a practice  or some sort of arrangement of things  like the three disciplines  you can approach stoicism think of it as  a building  that is you know vast in in its scope  and it has a number of different doors  you're not going to necessarily get  everything by going in one  door but you're not going to ruin  yourself  either by going in this door rather than  that door so  all that said by by way of preamble  let's break it down i would say that  there's there's three main  areas that you want to focus on  one of these is understanding what  stoicism is so  that's pretty important you know how are  you going to become  stoic whatever that means unless you  actually know  what stoicism stands for and and means  so there's no getting around that  another  really vital dimension i would say and  this is  this holds not just for stoicism but for  virtue ethics and philosophies  as ways of life in general practice  practice practice practice in the sense  of repeating it  and let's say practice in the plural  there are many different stoic practices  and you're going to want to start doing  some of them but not all of them as  we're going to explain  and then we can also talk about  direction orientation  motivation as being something important  as well  and there's there's a learning process  that takes place so let's talk about  understanding first now again  much that could be said about that i'm  going to try to condense this a little  bit  to understand what stoicism is so that  you have some  idea of what you're shooting at you  probably want to get rid of  any sort of preconceived notions that  you're bringing to it  as baggage so if you're associating  stoicism with  the stiff upper lip and not showing  emotion that's actually  wrong when it comes to classic stoicism  stoicism as a philosophy you want to get  rid of that  you want to if you can't get it out of  your mind at least as we say  put it in brackets right you have to be  open to learning what stoicism is  you want to actually i would say go to  the primary sources  we're fortunate in that we do have some  of the writings of the classical stoics  and they're available in a number of  translations some of them are more  old-fashioned some of them are more  contemporary  and more readable every one of you  who is watching this video you can in  fact  read seneca you can in fact read marcus  aurelius  you can in fact read epictetus those are  the big three  and you really do want to begin by  at least spending some time reading at  least a bit  of their work i always suggest beginning  with epictetus and caridian  and maybe marcus aurelius's meditations  and then going into the seneca and then  going back to the epictetus and then  reading other things as well but if you  just start with those  that can be quite helpful there's also  resources available  all over the internet to help you  understand these things  you know free courses videos podcasts  all that that sort of stuff and there's  nothing wrong with  using those sorts of resources but you  do want to spend some time  with the original text so that you're  getting it from the source  that doesn't mean that you can't read  you know things that are about stoicism  and are presenting it in in digested  form  or perhaps a more popular form  for you but you always have to be  careful it's sort of like  relying on somebody else there's a lot  of  let's just call it mediocre and  superficial  material out there on stoicism some of  it actually gets things wrong  and so you're not going to know who's  getting things right  unless you know who's reliable and the  index for reliability is of course  accordance with the the primary  teachings  and text so there are some you know good  secondary  sources out there but you've got to be a  bit careful so don't place  too much reliance on that i would say  anything that presents stoicism  as a life hack or you know the 10 easy  steps for stoicism  you want to be a bit leery of that sort  of stuff or  they're saying that all these different  historical figures are stoic  who aren't really affiliated with  stoicism  take that with the proverbial grain of  salt  i would say that you also want to if you  can  find a community to join in in there can  be virtual communities  like the various facebook groups that  are out there and  you know some of the people in those  groups are going to be at different  levels of expertise but you can find  some some decent stuff  in there you got to be careful about the  as i you know the sort of superficial  life hack stoicism  but you can find some really good  resources as well there are local  stoic communities that you can join in  and you can  you know bounce ideas off of other  people ask questions  many of them are available virtually due  to cobid19  and there's other things you can do you  can find somebody who's more experienced  to be kind of a coach or a guide for for  those sorts of things you can take  online  classes there are a number of them  available so all of that will help you  with  understanding what stoicism is  and then you can decide whether you  really want to be a stoic  or not second thing practice practice  is extraordinarily important because  this is not a theoretical philosophy  just by itself  practice is integral to stoicism and  practice and theory  have to be integrated within stoicism  and that's part of how you actually  learn  there are a lot of stoic practices out  there  i would suggest starting with one or two  not trying to as we say boil the ocean  just pick  one or two to actually play around with  on a daily basis  you might pick out a particular time of  day to work on say negative  visualization  or you might think about how you apply  them in actual  situations so when we do the practice of  you know taking a pause  and examining impressions that are  coming in or any of the other stoic  practices  there's the working on them like  rehearsal  and then there's the trying to actually  pull it off  and do it in real life situations and we  could find something kind of in between  as well we could do role playing or  stuff like that but you should you  should experiment with these practices  because  stoicism is a practical  philosophy and so now why begin with one  or two  because you want to get good at it right  if you're trying to learn an instrument  you don't try to do every single drill  and master every single technique all at  once  you go along kind of plotting bit by bit  by bit by bit and by the way this is  great  advice for any spiritual path  any intentional way of living any  philosophy of life you're going to see  that sort of you know  advice being given the other thing  that's important with practice  as you're starting out get yourself into  the habit  of paying attention to what you succeed  in  and what you fail in and and why  did you succeed in it because you  actually got it completely right or were  you just lucky  in this situation and you need to work  on it a bit more  did you fail because you didn't follow  through all the way did you fail because  some emotions arose and you allow those  to dominate  instead of rationality did you fail  because you became distracted  did you fail because you actually had  the wrong idea of what you were getting  into  these are all important considerations  the other things that i'll say about  beginning  as a stoic the biggest mistake that i've  seen  in years of talking with people who are  starting out  is expecting that because you have now  committed to this thing that you call  stoicism and you know something about  everybody else is to be assessed  on that basis and that is  a real danger you don't want to be  assuming  that just because you've had these great  insights that everybody else is having  them  and that they should be measured in  their thoughts and  actions and words and whatever else by  those things  because if you do that you're actually  setting yourself up for a lot  of grief and heartache and  misunderstandings  epictetus actually tells you that when  you start out don't tell anybody that  you're a stoic don't tell anybody here  that you're doing philosophy  just practice it keep working on it and  then after a while if people  ask you hey why do you seem not to lose  your temper so badly  that you can tell them right i've been  practicing stoicism and  it's been helping out i would say even  further don't try to evangelize  meaning don't spread the good news of  stoicism until you've got a solid basis  with it people get very exc